 "parsed": {
  "count": 2,
  "rationale": "Deploying mfa:admin and mfa:dbadmin together blocks exfiltration, and no single control does. Exfiltration needs admin on db, fileserver or backup. That needs a foothold there, and the only route is auth_db, auth_fileserver or auth_backup. With mfa_dbadmin deployed, those techniques also need admin_dc or admin_adfs. Getting either needs a foothold on dc or adfs via auth_dc or auth_adfs, which require NOT mfa_admin. So with both controls, no foothold on db, fileserver or backup is reachable. Why one control is not enough: phishing always gives foothold_workstation and stolen_user, so the attacker can pivot (if not segmented), authenticate to appserver and ciserver (MFA is bypassed from the workstation foothold), kerberoast for stolen_admin, and leak stolen_dbadmin from ciserver. Without mfa_dbadmin, auth_db then succeeds directly. Without mfa_admin, auth_dc then escalate_dc gives admin_dc, which bypasses mfa_dbadmin. Segmented alone fails because escalate_workstation gives admin_workstation, which still allows the pivot. Each patch alone leaves these paths open. A minimum of 2."
 },
